I will be sending out a more formal direction, tools, and methods to all bu=
t here are the high spots.

Your pitch is a little different depending on whether you are talking to a =
distributor or anyone else (owner, consultant, contractor).

This is all in the context that there may be site conditions that could fav=
or one or the other but for the general case this should hold true.

Owner/Consultant/Contractor/Distributor:

1.       PVC mains have given proven performance in the long term on golf c=
ourses in extreme applications such as regions where irrigation is required=
 year round.

2.       PE mains have not been used in great quantity for very long on gol=
f courses whether extreme or not.

3.       PE mains have yielded an incidence of failures related to workmans=
hip variability that PVC mains have never suffered.  Namely fusion joint fa=
ilures that do not manifest themselves except in time.

4.       Recent published work indicates that the PE fusion joints are not =
stronger than the pipe even when they are made well.  The proper phrasing i=
s fusion joints are not as long lived as the pipe.  The ability to predict =
this life does not exist and a golf irrigation system is the most extreme a=
pplication with high number of surges, high temperatures in the sunbelt, an=
d high pressures relative to the pipe typically used.

5.       PE may be a questionable selection in the extremes of golf in the =
sunbelt if municipal or reclaimed water is or could be used  because of its=
 vulnerability to oxidative degradation due to exposure to chlorine disinfe=
ctants and elevated temperatures.  PVC and DI are not susceptible to this p=
henomenon.

6.       PE fittings do not have pressure ratings that mean the same as the=
 pipe.  There are conventions of use but it is not known if the life of the=
 pipe or the fittings controls.  Like for PVC, PE fittings life in the pres=
ence of surges is shorter than for the pipe.  Methods do not exist to desig=
n for this effect.  DI fittings do not have this issue.

7.       PE mains are much more difficult, costly, and time consuming to re=
pair often requiring the hire of an outside contractor since specialized fu=
sion equipment and personnel training is required.  PVC mains are easily wo=
rked on with readily available material and existing staff.

8.       A repaired PE main is not as long lived as the original installati=
on because of the need to use electrofusion couplings which themselves are =
also subject to workmanship risk.  PVC mains repair are just as sound as th=
e original installation because the joining and parts are the same.

9.       PE mains repair are typically delayed because of the need to wait =
for specialized contractors whereas PVC can be repaired with readily availa=
ble staff and golf course staff.

10.   PVC laterals are prone to long term glue joint failures and PVC fitti=
ng failures due to the pounding from surges.  PE laterals will eliminate ma=
ny glue joints and have few fusion joints if pipe is bought in rolls.  If p=
olypropylene compression fittings are used most fusion joints are eliminate=
d.  Compression fittings are the standard fitting used in the joining of PE=
 pipe in irrigation in the rest of the world.

11.   PE laterals are easily maintained and repaired with compression fitti=
ngs by golf course staff.

12.   PVC systems are less costly to install in most circumstances.

Distribution:

13.   All of the above.

14.   PVC systems allow golf irrigation distributors a more defensible posi=
tion relative to outside distribution than PE systems do.  They won't be pr=
eyed on by Isco, CMF, Ferguson, and many others.

15.   PVC mains are the best choice for most customers and is overall the e=
thical choice.  It always pays to be right with your customer.



